<article class="env-remark" id="0D7K">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0D7K">Remark <span data-tag="0D7K">85.12.7</span> <span class="named">(Warning)</span>.</a>  Lemma <a href="/tag/0D7J" data-tag="0D7J">85.12.6</a> notwithstanding, it can happen that the category of cartesian $\mathcal{O}$-modules is abelian without being a Serre subcategory of $\textit{Mod}(\mathcal{O})$. Namely, suppose that we only know that $f_{\delta _1^1}$ and $f_{\delta _0^1}$ are flat. Then it follows easily from Lemma <a href="/tag/07TH" data-tag="07TH">85.12.5</a> that the category of cartesian $\mathcal{O}$-modules is abelian. But if $f_{\delta _0^2}$ is not flat (for example), there is no reason for the inclusion functor from the category of cartesian $\mathcal{O}$-modules to all $\mathcal{O}$-modules to be exact. </p>
</article>